+## TL;DR
+- `DocuSeal`, `OpenSign`, and `ContextGem` are the strongest open source starting points for contract workflows, intake packets, and structured document extraction.
+- `LexNLP`, `Blackstone`, `CourtListener`, and `eyecite` are the most directly legal-native repos in this list for extracting law-specific entities, citations, and court data.
+- `Docling`, `Unstructured`, `OCRmyPDF`, and `Whisper` are the practical document-ingestion layer for scanned PDFs, exhibits, and transcripts.
+- `Presidio` is the best privacy-first utility here for redaction, masking, and de-identification before sharing documents with an LLM.
+- `Haystack`, `LangChain`, `Flowise`, `AnythingLLM`, and `Qdrant` are the main self-hosted building blocks for firm-wide legal RAG and internal knowledge assistants.
+
+## Why This List
+Most "AI for lawyers" roundups are dominated by proprietary SaaS products, vague affiliate recommendations, or tools that are impossible to audit. This list takes the opposite approach: every project here is a public GitHub repository, which means law firms, legal ops teams, and solo practitioners can inspect code, self-host sensitive workloads, and build around tools that fit their own privacy, compliance, and retention policies.
+
+Open source matters more in legal workflows than in many other industries because the underlying data is often privileged, regulated, time-sensitive, or evidence-bearing. For the best AI tools for lawyers 2026, that means favoring repos that help with document ingestion, contract analysis, citation handling, privacy protection, and retrieval over internal legal knowledge. Where the legal-native open source ecosystem is thin, this list says so and pairs specialized legal repos with general-purpose self-hosted AI infrastructure that lawyers actually need in practice.
